# Back /var/log/journal with the persistent partition, before journald flushes.
#
# /var/log/journal EXISTS in the image, but it is on the read-only squashfs
# rootfs (/dev/root, 100% full by construction), so journald's Storage=auto
# decision came out "volatile": it wrote to /run/log/journal and every reboot
# destroyed the whole history. Measured identically on bench.local and
# cp-compute4.local — `journalctl --header` reported
# /run/log/journal/<machine-id>/system.journal and `journalctl --list-boots`
# showed exactly one boot. geekdojo/geekdojo-brain#601; the 22-hour firewall
# outage in #547 had to be diagnosed from live socket state because of it, and
# #500's eviction audit trail is a journald line, so that record was volatile
# too.
#
# THE PATTERN is rasputin-coredump-store.service's: bind-mount a
# persistent-partition directory over a baked, read-only mountpoint. A baked
# symlink cannot be used (it fails makedevs at build time, see that unit), and
# the mountpoint is already in the image, so nothing has to change in the build.
#
# THE ORDERING IS NOT THAT UNIT'S. rasputin-coredump-store.service is
# WantedBy=multi-user.target, which is far too late here: journald flushes
# /run -> /var exactly once, from systemd-journal-flush.service, and that unit
# runs in early boot (DefaultDependencies=no, Before=systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service,
# WantedBy=sysinit.target — read off cp-compute4). A mount that lands at
# multi-user.target arrives after the flush has already decided there is no
# persistent store, so the boot's own log — the part an investigation wants
# most — would still be lost. Hence:
#
#   DefaultDependencies=no       REQUIRED, not tidiness. A service with default
#                                dependencies gets After=sysinit.target, and
#                                systemd-journal-flush.service is ordered
#                                BEFORE systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service, which
#                                is part of sysinit.target. That is an ordering
#                                cycle, and systemd resolves a cycle by
#                                dropping an edge of its own choosing — so the
#                                unit would appear to work and then not.
#   RequiresMountsFor=           Requires + After var-lib-rasputin.mount. The
#                                store cannot exist before the partition does.
#   Before=systemd-journal-flush.service
#                                the whole point: the bind mount is in place
#                                before journald is told to flush.
#   WantedBy=sysinit.target      the same target systemd-journal-flush.service
#                                is installed into, so both jobs are in the
#                                initial boot transaction and the Before= edge
#                                above is what decides the order.
#
# THE COST, stated rather than discovered later. /var/lib/rasputin is `nofail`
# in fstab, so today nothing in the boot path waits for it (var-lib-rasputin.mount
# is WantedBy=local-fs.target with no Before=local-fs.target — measured). This
# unit puts that mount in front of systemd-journal-flush.service, and therefore
# in front of systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service and sysinit.target. On a node whose
# persistent device never appears, the boot now waits for the device unit to
# time out — DefaultDeviceTimeoutUSec=1min 30s on these nodes — before
# continuing. That is the price of never silently losing the journal again, and
# it is paid only by a node that has no identity, no join token and no console
# shadow either, i.e. one that is not serving anyway. If the mount fails the
# unit's job fails with it; Before= is ordering only, so the flush still
# proceeds and journald stays on /run exactly as it does today.
#
# NO ExecStop=umount, deliberately, and unlike the coredump unit.
# systemd-journal-flush.service is Conflicts=soft-reboot.target only (measured),
# so on an ordinary reboot its ExecStop=journalctl --smart-relinquish-var never
# runs and journald still holds system.journal open. An umount here would
# return EBUSY and leave this unit `failed`, which the QEMU smoke asserts is
# empty. Leaving the mount up is also the better behaviour: journald keeps
# writing persistently through the shutdown, so the shutdown is in the journal,
# and systemd-shutdown's own unmount sweep tears the bind mount down at the end
# the same way it tears down var-lib-rasputin.mount.
#
# Mode: see usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/zz-rasputin-journal.conf. The chmod below covers
# the window before systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service runs; that file is what
# keeps the mode 0700 afterwards, because stock systemd.conf resets
# /var/log/journal to 2755 root:systemd-journal on every boot.
[Unit]
Description=Persistent-partition backing store for the system journal
Documentation=man:systemd-journald.service(8) man:journald.conf(5)
DefaultDependencies=no
RequiresMountsFor=/var/lib/rasputin
ConditionPathIsMountPoint=/var/lib/rasputin
Before=systemd-journal-flush.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/bin/mkdir -p /var/lib/rasputin/journal
# 0700 root:root, unconditional rather than a mkdir mode: a directory left
# behind by an older image would keep whatever mode it already had. The journal
# is every log line on the node, which on this image includes the eviction
# audit trail (#500) and whatever a failing unit printed — nothing on the
# persistent partition that holds that kind of record is group- or
# world-reachable (see usr/lib/rasputin/atrest/inventory).
ExecStart=/bin/chmod 0700 /var/lib/rasputin/journal
# -o bind is understood by both busybox and util-linux mount.
ExecStart=/bin/mount -o bind /var/lib/rasputin/journal /var/log/journal

[Install]
WantedBy=sysinit.target
